BANK.push(
{"id":"g1","d":1,"q":"A multinational retailer is consolidating customer data from its EU, Brazilian, and US subsidiaries into one analytics platform in a US region of a public cloud provider. EU records include purchase histories linked to loyalty IDs. The provider holds ISO 27001 and SOC 2 Type II reports, procurement has already opened contract talks, and the CMO wants dashboards live within six months for the holiday season. As the security architect, what should you do FIRST?","o":["Work with legal and privacy to determine which laws apply to each data set, including transfer rules, and turn them into design requirements","Insert the EU standard contractual clauses into the provider agreement now, while procurement still has leverage in the negotiation","Pseudonymize loyalty IDs in each source region before export, so the analytics platform receives records with lower re-identification risk","Specify customer-managed keys in an HSM-backed key service for the US region so provider staff can't read records without the company's involvement"],"a":[0],"e":"Requirements come first. Until you know what GDPR, LGPD, and the relevant US state laws require for each data set, you can't pick a transfer mechanism or the right controls. SCCs may not even be the right tool if the provider is DPF certified. Pseudonymized data is still personal data under GDPR, so it doesn't settle the transfer question. Customer-managed keys may end up in the design, but they answer a requirement nobody has written yet. The provider's attestations are noise."},
{"id":"g2","d":1,"q":"A risk register shows a residual risk rated High for a legacy payment application that can't be patched until it is replaced in 18 months. Network isolation and enhanced monitoring were already factored in when the residual rating was set. The risk policy says High residual risks may be accepted only by the executive risk committee, which meets next month. The CIO, who owns the application budget, asks you to 'close out' the item before the board meeting. What is the MOST appropriate action?","o":["Have the CIO sign the acceptance as the accountable system owner, record the compensating controls, and set a quarterly review until replacement","Prepare an acceptance request for the executive risk committee with the residual rating, compensating controls, and an expiry tied to replacement","Re-score the risk with isolation and monitoring applied, and if it drops to Medium, close it through the normal owner-acceptance process","Put IPS virtual patching in front of the application to lower the residual risk further, then report the item to the board as mitigated at its meeting"],"a":[1],"e":"Policy puts acceptance of High residual risk with the executive risk committee, so the right move is a documented request to that body with an expiry tied to the replacement. The CIO doesn't have authority at this rating. Re-scoring double-counts controls already in the residual rating. Virtual patching may help, but reporting the risk as mitigated before it is re-assessed and accepted skips the governance step."},
{"id":"g3","d":1,"q":"A hospital has selected a SaaS vendor to run its new telehealth platform. The vendor will host video recordings of patient consultations in its own cloud tenancy. The vendor risk assessment is complete and found moderate residual risk, and counsel confirms the vendor will be a HIPAA business associate. Clinical leadership wants to start a pilot with 40 physicians and real patients in three weeks, and the vendor has offered free licenses for the pilot. What should the architect make sure happens NEXT?","o":["Arrange an independent penetration test of the vendor platform before the pilot, scoped to recording storage, the video session flow, and admin APIs","Execute a business associate agreement and a contract security schedule covering safeguards, audit rights, breach notice, and data return","Integrate the platform with hospital SSO and MFA so physician access follows existing joiner, mover, and leaver controls from day one","Request the vendor's HITRUST or SOC 2 Type II report and compare its control scope against the HIPAA Security Rule requirements"],"a":[1],"e":"HIPAA requires a BAA before a business associate handles PHI, and a pilot with real patients means PHI. The contract is also where audit rights, breach timing, and data return get fixed. The assessment is already done, so another attestation review adds little. Testing the vendor's platform needs their contractual permission and isn't the gating item. SSO is good hygiene but doesn't meet the legal prerequisite."},
{"id":"g4","d":1,"q":"During a design review for a new data lake, you read that raw order data from EU customers, collected under contract for fulfillment and delivery, will be kept for the company's existing six-year tax retention period. The business case adds that the same raw records will also feed a shared model training pool 'for future products not yet defined.' The lake will use encryption at rest and role-based access, and the DPO hasn't reviewed the proposal yet. Which GDPR principle is MOST directly challenged by this design?","o":["Storage limitation, because raw records stay identifiable for six years when a shorter period could serve fulfillment","Data minimisation, because raw records carry more fields than a training use case is likely to need","Purpose limitation, because data collected for fulfillment is being reused for purposes that aren't yet specified","Accountability, because the DPO hasn't reviewed the design and the controller can't yet demonstrate compliance"],"a":[2],"e":"The retention period is defined and tied to a legal obligation, so storage limitation isn't the core problem. Reuse for undefined future purposes can't be shown to be compatible with the original purpose, which is a purpose limitation failure. Minimisation is judged against a purpose, so it can't be assessed until the purpose is fixed. The missing DPO review is a process gap, not the principle the design breaks. Encryption doesn't change any of this."},
{"id":"g5","d":1,"q":"The board of a mid-sized insurer wants a single view of cybersecurity outcomes, including governance and supply chain risk, that shows where each business unit is today and where it should be in two years. The CISO wants the view to drive a prioritized gap plan rather than produce a certificate. One subsidiary is already certified to ISO 27001:2022, and IT operations uses COBIT 2019 for IT process governance. Which approach BEST fits the board's request?","o":["Extend the subsidiary's ISO 27001 scope to the whole group and report Statement of Applicability status to the board quarterly","Build NIST CSF 2.0 current and target profiles for each business unit and use the gaps between them to drive the roadmap","Adopt CIS Controls v8 and report each unit's progress through the implementation groups as its current and target position","Extend COBIT 2019 capability assessments to the security processes and report current and target capability levels"],"a":[1],"e":"CSF 2.0 profiles are built for this: outcome-based current and target states, including the Govern function and supply chain risk, with gaps that feed a prioritized plan. ISO 27001 is a certifiable management system and the SoA shows control applicability, not a maturity gap. CIS implementation groups prioritize technical safeguards by organization size, not governance outcomes. COBIT covers IT process capability broadly and is weaker on cybersecurity outcomes."},
{"id":"g6","d":1,"q":"A retailer runs one e-commerce platform in three countries. Country A's law requires that personal data of its citizens be stored only within its borders, though it allows processing support from abroad under contract. Countries B and C have no such rule. The business wants one global order management platform, finance has rejected the cost of a fully separate stack, and the platform team recently moved to Kubernetes. Which design BEST meets the requirement?","o":["Keep one global data store and encrypt Country A records with keys held in an HSM physically inside Country A, so foreign staff can't read them","Run a complete separate instance of the platform in Country A, with its own database, operations team, and release pipeline","Store Country A personal data in an in-country data store, and have the global platform reference it through tokens and local services","Store Country A data in-country and replicate a pseudonymized copy to the global store so that central analytics keeps working"],"a":[2],"e":"Partitioning keeps Country A's personal data stored in-country while the global platform works with tokens and calls local services, which keeps one platform at a reasonable cost. Encrypted data held abroad is still stored abroad, so local key custody doesn't meet a storage rule. A full separate stack works but finance has rejected it. A pseudonymized copy is still personal data and would sit outside the country. Kubernetes is noise."},
{"id":"g7","d":1,"q":"The board has approved a risk appetite statement of 'low appetite for risks that could cause regulatory sanction.' A product team plans to launch a new mobile feature in four weeks with a known gap in consent capture for marketing cookies in two EU markets. The product VP, who can accept Medium risks, has signed an acceptance. The security team rated the gap High, and marketing projects $3M in first-year revenue. What should the architect do?","o":["Escalate through the risk governance process with the rating, the appetite conflict, and options such as a delay or a consent fix","Accept the VP's sign-off but add a 60-day remediation plan with a named owner, and record the gap in the risk register for tracking","Ask engineering to switch off the feature in the two EU markets and launch it elsewhere on schedule while the consent flow is fixed","Notify the privacy office and the DPO so they can decide whether the gap needs to be reported to the lead supervisory authority"],"a":[0],"e":"The risk is rated High and conflicts with stated appetite, and the VP's authority stops at Medium, so the decision belongs higher up. The architect's job is to escalate with clear options. Accepting the VP's sign-off ignores the authority limit. Geofencing could well be one of the options, but it's a business decision the architect shouldn't make alone. Nothing has been processed yet, so there is no breach to report; involving the DPO is useful but not the decision path."},
{"id":"g8","d":1,"q":"A company is subject to PCI DSS 4.0 and SOX. Internal audit, the QSA, and the external financial auditor each test the same ERP access control on separate schedules, and control owners say they spend weeks a year producing near-identical evidence. Budget for a GRC platform was approved last quarter, and the ERP is moving to a new hosting provider next year. Which approach BEST addresses the duplication?","o":["Certify the ERP environment to ISO 27001 so that one external certificate can be offered to each of the three assessors as evidence of operating controls","Automate evidence collection for the ERP access control in the GRC platform so owners can export the same package for each request","Ask the external auditor to rely on the QSA's report on compliance for the access control instead of performing separate testing","Build a common control framework that maps each control to all applicable requirements, test once, and agree evidence reuse with assessors"],"a":[3],"e":"A common control framework lets one test satisfy several requirements, and agreeing reuse with the assessors up front is what actually stops repeat testing. ISO 27001 certification doesn't replace PCI or SOX testing. Automating evidence cuts effort but still leaves three separate tests. The financial auditor relies on its own or internal audit work under its standards, and a PCI ROC isn't scoped for SOX, so that request is unlikely to work on its own."},
{"id":"g9","d":1,"q":"A regional data center has an asset value of $4,000,000. A flood is expected to destroy 25% of its value and is expected once every 20 years. The facility supports $12M in annual revenue and carries property insurance with a $250,000 deductible. A flood barrier would reduce the exposure factor to 5% and costs $25,000 per year to maintain. Using standard quantitative risk analysis and ignoring the insurance, what is the annual value of the barrier?","o":["$40,000 per year","$15,000 a year","$25,000 per annum","$7,500 a year"],"a":[1],"e":"SLE before = 4,000,000 x 0.25 = 1,000,000. ARO = 1/20 = 0.05, so ALE before = 50,000. SLE after = 4,000,000 x 0.05 = 200,000, so ALE after = 10,000. Value = 50,000 - 10,000 - 25,000 = $15,000. $40,000 forgets the cost of the control, $25,000 subtracts the cost from the original ALE without the new ALE, and $7,500 has no valid basis. Revenue and the deductible are noise."},
{"id":"g10","d":1,"q":"After a merger, Company A requires 15-character passphrases with no forced expiry, breached-password screening, and MFA for all remote access. Company B requires 8-character complex passwords rotated every 60 days, and about 30% of its users have no MFA. Both companies have card data environments in PCI DSS 4.0 scope. The CISO asks the architect to propose one harmonized password standard within a quarter. Which approach is BEST?","o":["Combine the strictest settings from each policy, 15-character minimum length with 60-day rotation, so the merged standard is at least as strong as either","Keep both policies until identity platforms are merged in 12 months, then decide based on whichever directory becomes the system of record","Base it on current guidance such as NIST SP 800-63B and the combined risk, adopt A's model, and make MFA coverage a condition for dropping rotation","Adopt Company B's policy across the group, because periodic rotation limits how long a stolen credential stays useful to an attacker"],"a":[2],"e":"Standards should rest on risk and current guidance. NIST 800-63B favors length and breach screening over forced rotation, and PCI DSS 4.0 only requires 90-day changes where a password is the sole factor, so extending MFA is what makes dropping rotation defensible. Stacking long passwords with frequent rotation pushes users toward predictable patterns. Waiting a year leaves two standards in place. Rotation alone is weaker than length plus screening plus MFA."},
{"id":"g11","d":1,"q":"An architecture governance board has approved 140 exceptions to security standards over three years. Audit found that 90 are past their review date, many have owners who left the company, and some compensating controls were never implemented. The CISO wants a fix that the board can sustain without adding headcount. A new ITSM platform with workflow automation goes live next month. Which change will do the MOST to keep exceptions from becoming permanent?","o":["Require each new exception to be approved by the CISO personally, so that a single senior person sees, challenges, and signs off on the full population","Make exceptions time-bound with a named owner and verified compensating controls, and have the workflow expire or escalate them at the end date","Run a one-time cleanup that closes or renews each of the 90 overdue exceptions and reassigns orphaned ones to current managers","Publish an exceptions dashboard to business unit leaders each month showing counts and ages, so peer pressure drives closure"],"a":[1],"e":"The root cause is that exceptions have no enforced end. Time limits, a named owner, verified compensating controls, and automatic expiry or escalation fix that, and the new workflow tool means no extra headcount. CISO approval adds a bottleneck but doesn't force review. A cleanup fixes today's backlog and the problem returns. A dashboard helps visibility but nothing makes an exception end."},
{"id":"g12","d":1,"q":"A company plans to move its HR system to a SaaS provider. The provider offers a SOC 2 Type I report dated last month covering security and confidentiality, plus a penetration test summary from a reputable firm. Procurement says this is good enough because 'it's a SOC 2.' Go-live is in four months, and the HR data includes salary and medical leave records. What is the MOST important limitation for the architect to raise?","o":["The report covers only the security and confidentiality criteria, so availability and processing integrity of payroll aren't addressed","A Type I report was issued by the provider's chosen auditor, so the company should commission its own independent assessment instead","The pen test summary describes findings at one point in time and doesn't show whether the issues it found have since been remediated","A Type I report evaluates control design on a single date and doesn't test whether controls operated effectively over a review period"],"a":[3],"e":"The key gap is that Type I tests design on a date, not operating effectiveness over time, so it says little about whether controls actually work. Ask for a Type II or a committed date for one. Criteria scope matters, but missing availability is secondary to having no evidence of operation at all. Every SOC 2 is commissioned by the service organization, which is normal. Pen test remediation is worth asking about but is a narrower point."},
{"id":"g13","d":1,"q":"A US manufacturer is finalizing firmware for industrial controllers that will use AES-256 for field-bus encryption. Sales already has orders from Germany, Brazil, and a customer in a country under comprehensive US sanctions that was routed through a distributor in a third country. Engineering has passed FIPS 140-3 validation for the module, and the release date is in six weeks. What should the security architect raise BEFORE the design is finalized?","o":["Whether the FIPS 140-3 validation certificate also covers the firmware version that is going to ship, since a change could void it","Export control classification of the product and the sanctions screening of end users, including orders that arrive through distributors","Whether key management in the field will let customers rotate keys without a firmware update, given the long service life of the controllers","Whether the German and Brazilian customers have their own regulatory requirements for encryption used in operational technology environments"],"a":[1],"e":"Cryptographic products need an export classification under the EAR, and an end user in a sanctioned country is a legal stop regardless of the route through a distributor. That has to be resolved before shipping. FIPS coverage of the shipping version matters for some buyers but isn't the legal gate. Field key rotation is a sound design point but not the most urgent. Customer-side OT rules are the customers' concern and come later."},
{"id":"g14","d":1,"q":"A business impact analysis shows a claims processing system has a maximum tolerable downtime of 48 hours. The current backup design restores it in about 72 hours, based on a test run last quarter. The claims VP proposes simply accepting the gap because 'we've never had an outage that long.' The infrastructure team has quoted $180K a year for warm standby, and the DR runbook hasn't been updated since a database upgrade. What should the architect do FIRST?","o":["Update the DR runbook for the new database version and rerun the restore test to confirm the actual recovery time before deciding anything","Present the gap as a risk with options and costs to close it, and have the business owner decide formally at the right authority level","Approve the warm standby design, since the BIA sets 48 hours as the limit and the current design clearly can't meet that requirement","Record the VP's acceptance in the risk register with the outage history as rationale, and schedule a review at the next BIA cycle"],"a":[1],"e":"The business owns the decision, but it needs to be made formally with the gap, options, and costs in front of the right authority. Rerunning the test is useful, but the last test already showed 72 hours against a 48-hour limit, so the gap exists either way. Approving spend isn't the architect's call. Recording an informal acceptance based on outage history skips the analysis and may sit above the VP's authority."},
{"id":"g15","d":1,"q":"A bank must choose between two designs, each with a five-year life and no residual value. Design A costs $2M up front and reduces annualized loss expectancy from $1.2M to $300K. Design B costs $500K up front and reduces ALE from $1.2M to $700K. Design A also needs a specialist the bank would have to hire, though HR says the role is fillable. Ignoring the time value of money, which design delivers the greater total net benefit over its life, and by how much?","o":["Design B, by $1.5M, because its return on investment is 400% compared with Design A's 125%","Design A, by $500K of total net benefit across the five-year life","Design B, by $500K, because it delivers its benefit for a quarter of the up-front cost","Neither, since each returns about $2M of net benefit over five years"],"a":[1],"e":"A: reduction of $900K x 5 = $4.5M, minus $2M = $2.5M net. B: $500K x 5 = $2.5M, minus $0.5M = $2.0M net. A wins by $500K. B has the better ROI ratio (400% vs 125%), which is why those distractors are tempting, but the question asks about total net benefit, and capital isn't stated as constrained. They aren't equal. The hiring point is noise since the role is fillable."},
{"id":"g16","d":1,"q":"A product owner is launching a customer portal that will store scanned identity documents. The security architect's review found that the chosen design leaves a moderate residual risk of document exposure through an admin API. The product owner, under a tight launch date, emails the architect: 'You know the tech better than anyone, so just tell me whether we can accept this.' The CISO is traveling, and internal audit has asked to be copied on major risk decisions. What response BEST reflects the architect's role?","o":["Make the acceptance call, since the architect has the deepest technical understanding of the risk, and record it in the register","Lay out design options with their residual risk, cost, and schedule impact, then let the product owner decide and implement the choice","Refer the decision to internal audit so an independent party makes the acceptance call and the product owner isn't marking their own work","Rate the risk and log it in the register, and leave treatment decisions to the CISO's governance team when the CISO is back"],"a":[1],"e":"Architects advise on risk and translate the owner's decision into design. The owner, who is accountable for the business outcome, makes the call. Accepting the risk yourself takes an authority you don't have. Internal audit is the third line and shouldn't make management decisions or it loses independence. Logging and waiting is too passive when the owner needs options now."},
{"id":"g17","d":1,"q":"A company uses a cloud provider's managed relational database for regulated customer records. A regulator requires the company to show that encryption keys for this data are under its exclusive control and that it can make the data unreadable to the provider on demand. The database must keep running queries and indexes on the regulated columns, and the team has six months. The provider offers a native KMS, BYOK import, and an external key store option. Which design BEST satisfies the requirement?","o":["Encrypt the regulated columns in the application before writing them, so the database stores only ciphertext the provider can't read","Generate keys on-premises and import them into the provider's KMS through BYOK, so the company controls key creation and rotation","Use the provider's native KMS with customer-managed keys and a key policy that denies provider administrators decrypt rights","Use the external key store option backed by a company-operated HSM, so the company holds the keys and can revoke access at any time"],"a":[3],"e":"An external key store keeps key material in the company's own HSM, and revoking access makes the data unreadable to the provider while the managed service still works. Client-side column encryption gives strong control but breaks the required queries and indexes. BYOK still leaves key material inside the provider's KMS. A key policy on the native KMS is a provider-run control, so it doesn't show exclusive control."},
{"id":"g18","d":1,"q":"An organization is preparing for its ISO/IEC 27001:2022 stage 2 certification audit. The auditor's plan says the team will sample how controls were selected and will ask why several Annex A controls, including physical security monitoring and secure coding, were left out. The ISMS scope covers a cloud-only SaaS business with no development team. The risk team keeps a detailed risk register in a GRC tool. Which document will the auditor examine FIRST for this line of questioning?","o":["The risk treatment plan, which records how each identified risk will be treated and who owns each treatment","The Statement of Applicability, which lists each Annex A control, whether it's included, and the justification","The ISMS scope statement, which explains the boundaries of the management system and its external interfaces","The risk assessment report, which shows the identified risks, their likelihood and impact, and the resulting ratings"],"a":[1],"e":"Clause 6.1.3 requires a Statement of Applicability listing every Annex A control, whether it's included, and why any are excluded. That's the exact question the auditor is asking. The treatment plan and assessment report support the SoA but don't hold exclusion justifications. The scope statement explains boundaries, which may explain why secure coding is out, but the auditor checks the SoA for the stated reason."},
{"id":"g19","d":1,"q":"A global company wants one data classification scheme. Business units now use 'Confidential,' 'Restricted,' 'Internal Only,' and 'Secret' with different meanings in each region. The company just bought Microsoft 365 E5 licenses that include automatic sensitivity labeling, and the CIO wants labels applied to all SharePoint content within the quarter. Legal says two regions have regulator-defined categories for personal data. Which step should come FIRST?","o":["Define the levels by business impact of disclosure, alteration, or loss, with criteria that cover the regulator-defined categories","Run discovery across SharePoint and file shares to measure how much content sits in each existing label, so the new scheme fits actual volumes","Map each business unit's existing labels to a proposed set of four levels so migration can start while definitions are refined","Configure auto-labeling policies for the most common sensitive data types now so protection begins while the scheme is agreed"],"a":[0],"e":"Labels are only meaningful once the levels and their criteria are agreed, and those criteria need to include the regulator-defined categories. Discovery helps plan the rollout but doesn't define the scheme. Mapping old labels to new levels requires the new levels to be defined first. Auto-labeling before definitions exist builds rework into the tool and may label data inconsistently."},
{"id":"g20","d":1,"q":"A medical device maker depends on a single vendor for an embedded communications module. An assessment finds the vendor has no secure development program, no SBOM, and slow patching. No other supplier is qualified, and building an in-house module would take about three years. The vendor will accept new contract terms but can't afford a full SDLC program this year. Product launch is in nine months. Which risk treatment is MOST appropriate?","o":["Avoid the risk by starting an in-house module program now and delaying the product launch until the replacement is qualified","Accept the risk formally, since there's no alternative supplier, and record the vendor gaps in the register for annual review","Transfer the risk through product liability and cyber insurance that covers losses from defects in third-party components","Mitigate through contract terms such as SBOM delivery and patch timelines, plus independent testing and isolation of the module"],"a":[3],"e":"Mitigation is available and affordable: contract for an SBOM and patch timelines, test the module independently, and isolate it in the design. Avoidance costs three years and the launch. Formal acceptance is premature when practical mitigations exist. Insurance can cover some financial loss but not patient safety, regulatory exposure, or the vendor's weak practices."},
{"id":"g21","d":1,"q":"A retailer is designing a data-sharing service for California consumers. The service will sell segment-level insights to ad partners, and each segment record carries hashed email addresses so partners can match against their own customers. Marketing argues the data is 'aggregated' and so falls outside the CCPA as amended by the CPRA. The retailer already honors deletion requests within 45 days. Which requirement MOST directly applies to the new service?","o":["Collect opt-in consent before selling a consumer's data, and record the consent with a timestamp for later audit","Let consumers opt out of the sale or sharing of their data, and honor opt-out preference signals such as GPC","Treat the segments as aggregate consumer information, and document the aggregation method to show it isn't personal","Store California consumer data in US regions, and restrict partner access to US-based processing locations"],"a":[1],"e":"Hashed emails are persistent identifiers, so the segments are personal information and the service is a sale. The CCPA requires a right to opt out of sale and sharing, and opt-out preference signals like GPC must be honored. Opt-in is required only for consumers under 16. The data isn't truly aggregate because it can be matched to individuals. The CCPA has no data localization rule."},
{"id":"g22","d":1,"q":"A regional bank is deciding whether to build a 24x7 security operations center or buy a managed detection and response service. The managed option is 40% cheaper over five years and the provider covers 300 other banks. The bank's regulator expects the board to oversee material outsourcing, and the bank's incident response plan names the CISO as the decision maker for customer notification. From a governance view, which factor matters MOST?","o":["Whether the five-year total cost, including integration and exit costs, still favors buying once hidden costs such as exit fees are counted","Whether the provider's detection coverage and analyst ratios are at least as strong as what an in-house team could deliver","Whether the bank can still meet its accountability duties and oversee the provider through contract terms, reporting, and audit","Whether the provider can integrate with the bank's SIEM and ticketing so alerts reach the bank's responders without delay"],"a":[2],"e":"Accountability can't be outsourced. The bank must still oversee the provider, meet the regulator's outsourcing expectations, and keep the CISO's notification decision working. Cost, detection quality, and integration all matter to the decision, but they're financial and operational factors, not governance."},
{"id":"g23","d":1,"q":"During a risk workshop at a logistics company, stakeholders disagree on how likely a disruptive ransomware attack is, with estimates from 'once in ten years' to 'twice a year.' The company has little internal incident history. The CFO wants a number that can be compared with insurance premiums, and the risk team owns a Monte Carlo tool that hasn't been used yet. Which approach should the architect use FIRST to arrive at a defensible likelihood?","o":["Feed the full spread of stakeholder estimates into the Monte Carlo tool as the input range so the output reflects the uncertainty","Use structured expert elicitation with calibrated estimators and industry incident data to produce a justified likelihood range","Take the most pessimistic estimate as the planning value, since conservative assumptions are prudent for a disruptive scenario","Average the stakeholder estimates to get a central value and document the range of views as a note in the risk register"],"a":[1],"e":"With little internal data, the defensible step is structured elicitation, such as calibrated estimates or Delphi, anchored to external incident data. Its output can then feed the Monte Carlo tool. Feeding uncalibrated opinions into a simulation gives a precise-looking but weak result. The worst case biases decisions and premium comparisons. Averaging hides the reasons people disagree."},
{"id":"g24","d":1,"q":"A company is acquiring a 600-person firm. Leadership needs the target's staff on the parent's email and ERP on day one, which is two weeks after close. Due diligence found unpatched servers, no asset inventory, and a flat network. The parent's EDR licenses can cover the target, but deployment will take about six weeks. The integration budget was set before diligence finished. What should the architect recommend FIRST?","o":["Delay connecting the two networks until a full asset inventory is built and the critical vulnerabilities on the target's servers are fixed and verified","Treat the target network as untrusted and provide day-one access through controlled interfaces, with baselines required before wider links","Commission a penetration test of the target network before close to size the remediation effort and update the integration budget","Start the EDR rollout across the target's endpoints now and connect the networks once EDR coverage reaches an agreed percentage"],"a":[1],"e":"Day-one access is a business requirement, so the first move is to give it in a way that contains risk: treat the target as untrusted and expose only what's needed through controlled interfaces, with baselines as the gate for deeper integration. Delaying until full remediation misses the business date. A pen test can't happen before close and doesn't deliver access. EDR is part of the plan but takes six weeks, so it can't be the day-one control."},
{"id":"g25","d":1,"q":"A new rule requires financial firms to report material cyber incidents to the regulator within 72 hours of determining that an incident is material. A recent tabletop at one firm showed the SOC detected the simulated intrusion within an hour, but nobody could say who decides materiality, and the question bounced between legal, risk, and IT for four days. The firm has a mature SIEM and a forensic retainer. Which capability should the architect prioritize?","o":["Pre-approved notification templates and a regulator contact list so the report can be filed quickly once a decision is made","Longer SIEM log retention and centralized evidence storage so the firm has the facts needed to assess materiality","A defined materiality process with set criteria, named decision makers, and an escalation path from detection to them","A larger forensic retainer with contracted response times so investigators can scope the incident faster after detection"],"a":[2],"e":"The tabletop shows detection works and the gap is the decision: no criteria and no named decision maker. A defined materiality process with escalation fixes that. Templates help only after the decision. More log retention and forensic capacity speed up fact-finding, but facts weren't the bottleneck in the tabletop."},
{"id":"g26","d":1,"q":"A security architect is building a business case for a $1.2M privileged access management platform. The company has no open audit findings on privileged access. In the last review, executives asked 'what do we get for this money?' and lost interest when the team showed vendor feature lists. Peer benchmarks show the company spends less on security than similar firms, and the vendor has supplied an ROI calculator. Which approach will MOST effectively show value to the executives?","o":["Show that spending is below peer benchmarks, so the investment brings the company in line with similar organizations","Tie the platform to the business risks it reduces, using expected loss reduction and the impact on key operations","Present the count of unmanaged privileged accounts and shared passwords, with the trend over the last four quarters","Use the vendor's ROI calculator to show payback period and savings from automating password rotation and reviews"],"a":[1],"e":"Executives fund reductions in business risk, so express value in their terms: expected loss reduction and protection of key operations. Benchmarks show spending, not value. Account counts are technical metrics that need translation to matter to executives. A vendor ROI calculator has obvious bias and focuses on efficiency rather than risk."},
{"id":"g27","d":1,"q":"An organization keeps seven years of backup tapes with a third-party vault provider to meet financial record retention rules. A new privacy regulation requires that a person's personal data be erased on request within 30 days, and its guidance says suppression alone doesn't count as erasure. Restoring and rewriting tapes is impractical. Backup software is being replaced next year, and the vault contract renews in six months. Which approach BEST meets the requirement going forward?","o":["Keep a suppression list of erased subjects and reapply deletions automatically whenever a backup is restored to production","Cut tape retention to 30 days so an erased person's data ages out of backups within the regulatory window","Encrypt each person's data with its own key in the new backup design and destroy that key when erasure is requested","Move backups to the vault provider's cloud service, where individual records can be found and deleted within the backup set"],"a":[2],"e":"Per-subject keys let you make one person's data unreadable across every backup by destroying the key, which counts as erasure in practice. A suppression list is a good interim measure, but the regulator has said it isn't erasure. Cutting retention to 30 days breaks the seven-year financial retention rule. Record-level deletion in backups is uncommon and would mean rewriting the backup sets, which is the impractical step."},
{"id":"g28","d":1,"q":"Legal is preparing for possible product defect litigation and has issued a hold covering five years of communications from 35 named engineering custodians. The email platform automatically deletes messages older than 90 days under the retention policy, and the next purge job runs tonight. The privacy office worries about over-retention, and the eDiscovery tool license covers only part of the company. What should the architect do FIRST?","o":["Suspend automatic deletion for the named custodians and preserve their data in place with integrity and chain of custody","Turn off the 90-day deletion policy across the whole tenant until legal confirms the final scope and custodian list for the hold","Export each custodian's mailbox to offline archive files and store them in a restricted share managed by the legal team","Send each custodian a hold notice asking them to keep relevant messages and stop deleting anything until further notice"],"a":[0],"e":"A targeted in-place hold on the named custodians stops tonight's purge for the right data and keeps it defensible. Disabling deletion tenant-wide over-preserves and conflicts with the privacy office's concern. Exports to archive files are weaker for integrity and chain of custody. Custodian notices are needed too, but they don't stop an automated purge."},
{"id":"g29","d":1,"q":"A merchant's card data flows through 40 systems: the e-commerce site, call center desktops, order management, the data warehouse, and several batch jobs. Its PCI DSS 4.0 assessment costs keep rising, and the QSA says nearly the whole data center is in scope. The network team has proposed a $400K segmentation project. The business uses card numbers only for authorization, refunds, and chargebacks. Which approach will do the MOST to reduce assessment scope?","o":["Segment the network so that only the 40 systems that handle card data remain in the cardholder data environment","Replace card numbers with tokens and use P2PE at capture, so most of the systems stop storing or handling card data","Encrypt card numbers at rest in the warehouse and order systems with strong key management and documented key custody","Move the 40 systems into a dedicated cloud account with its own controls, so the rest of the estate falls outside scope"],"a":[1],"e":"The business doesn't need card numbers beyond authorization, refunds, and chargebacks, which tokens support. Tokenization and P2PE remove card data from most of the 40 systems, which shrinks scope the most. Segmentation only fences the 40 systems, and they stay in scope. Encrypted card data is still in scope where keys are accessible. A separate cloud account is a form of segmentation with the same limit."},
{"id":"g30","d":1,"q":"An EU company plans to use a US-based SaaS provider for customer support ticketing. The provider is self-certified under the EU-US Data Privacy Framework and lists subprocessors in the US and India. The DPO has asked whether anything else is needed before signing, and procurement wants to close this month. Tickets often contain account numbers and, now and then, health details that customers volunteer. Which statement is MOST accurate?","o":["DPF gives a valid transfer basis for the US provider, but an Article 28 agreement and the controller's own duties still apply","DPF certification removes the need for a processing agreement, since the provider has already committed to the DPF principles","DPF transfers need the same transfer impact assessment as SCCs, so one must be completed before the provider can be used","DPF certification covers the provider's subprocessors in India as well, since onward transfers fall under the provider's own certification"],"a":[0],"e":"DPF is an adequacy-based transfer mechanism for certified US organizations, but the controller still needs an Article 28 agreement and keeps its own duties. It doesn't replace the processing agreement. Adequacy-based transfers don't need a TIA the way SCC transfers do. Onward transfers to India need their own safeguards, such as SCCs in the subprocessor chain."},
{"id":"g31","d":1,"q":"After a breach at an online pharmacy, plaintiffs argue the company was negligent. The company points to several things it did. Before signing with its hosting provider, it reviewed the provider's SOC 2 report and pen test results. It bought a cyber insurance policy. Its security team subscribed to sector threat intelligence. And for three years it ran monthly patching, MFA on admin access, and log monitoring, all with records showing they operated. Which of these BEST demonstrates due care?","o":["Reviewing the hosting provider's SOC 2 report and pen test results before signing the hosting contract","Running patching, admin MFA, and log monitoring for three years with records showing they operated","Buying a cyber insurance policy sized to the company's estimated exposure from a data breach","Subscribing to sector threat intelligence so the security team understood the threats it faced"],"a":[1],"e":"Due care is doing what a prudent organization would do: putting reasonable controls in place and actually operating them, with evidence. Reviewing the provider before contracting and studying the threat landscape are due diligence, the investigation that informs decisions. Insurance transfers financial loss but doesn't protect anyone."},
{"id":"g32","d":1,"q":"A manufacturer asks the architect to design a third-party risk program covering 2,000 suppliers. The team has four analysts. Roughly 150 suppliers have network connections or handle sensitive data, about 30 are single-source for critical parts, and the top 50 by spend are mostly raw material vendors with no system access. A security ratings service subscription is already paid for. Which design is MOST effective?","o":["Send a standard security questionnaire to all 2,000 suppliers each year and have the analysts follow up on the riskiest answers","Tier suppliers by inherent risk such as data access, connectivity, and criticality, and scale the depth of assessment to the tier","Monitor all 2,000 suppliers with the ratings service and open a deeper review only when a supplier's score drops below a threshold","Do full assessments of the top 50 suppliers by annual spend each year and rely on contract clauses to cover the rest of the base"],"a":[1],"e":"Tiering by inherent risk puts four analysts on the roughly 150 connected or data-handling suppliers and the 30 critical ones, with lighter checks elsewhere. Questionnaires for all 2,000 swamp the team and are shallow everywhere. Ratings services give an outside-in view and are a useful input, not a program. Spend doesn't track risk here, since the top spenders have no system access."},
{"id":"g33","d":1,"q":"An architect finds that a regional sales unit has been running a cloud scheduling app for six months that stores patient appointment details, which are regulated health data. Security and legal weren't involved, there's no contract beyond click-through terms, and the app supports about $8M a year in bookings. Logs show no sign of compromise, and the vendor appears reputable. What should the architect do FIRST?","o":["Block the app at the CASB and web proxy until a full review is complete, then restore access if it passes","Report the unapproved processing to the health regulator now, since patient data has been processed for six months without a contract in place","Assess the app's risk and the data involved with the business owner, then bring it under governance with a contract and controls","Move the appointment data into the company's approved scheduling platform and decommission the app over the next month"],"a":[2],"e":"With no sign of compromise and real revenue at stake, the first step is to understand the risk with the business owner and bring the app under governance, including a proper contract. Blocking it immediately hurts revenue without knowing the risk, though it may be right if the assessment finds something serious. Unapproved processing isn't automatically a reportable breach. Migration may be the end result, but it's a decision to make after the assessment."},
{"id":"g34","d":1,"q":"A company's cyber insurer requires MFA on all remote access and email as a condition of coverage. Last month the CFO signed the renewal application stating that MFA is fully enforced. The architect now finds that 15% of users, mostly executives and field staff, have exemptions. The VPN supports MFA, and the help desk says enforcing it for field staff will take about eight weeks. Which risk should the architect highlight to the CFO FIRST?","o":["The exempted accounts are more exposed to phishing and credential stuffing, so the likelihood of a successful intrusion is higher","The insurer could deny a related claim or rescind the policy because the application misstated a control it relied on","Premiums could rise sharply at the next renewal if the insurer's scanning finds remote access without MFA in place","Regulators could treat the exemptions as a failure of reasonable security if a breach occurs through one of these accounts"],"a":[1],"e":"The CFO signed an application stating MFA is fully enforced. That misstatement can void coverage or justify a claim denial exactly when the company needs it, so it's the most urgent risk to raise, along with correcting the record with the insurer. Higher intrusion likelihood is real but is the general risk, not the CFO-specific one. Premium increases and regulator views are secondary."},
{"id":"g35","d":1,"q":"The board asks the CISO for one key risk indicator that shows whether the security program is reducing risk over time. The SOC already reports blocked attacks and mean time to detect, IT reports patch compliance within SLA, and the risk team keeps a register of risks scored against the board-approved appetite. Board members have said they don't understand the operational charts. Which indicator is MOST meaningful for this purpose?","o":["Percentage of critical patches applied within SLA, trended monthly against the target set in the standard","Mean time to detect confirmed incidents, trended quarterly against the industry benchmark the SOC uses for reporting","Number of risks to critical business services rated above the board's approved appetite, trended over time","Number of attacks blocked at the perimeter and email gateway each month, with the year-over-year change"],"a":[2],"e":"The board set the appetite, so the trend of critical risks above it shows directly whether the program is reducing risk where it matters. Patch compliance and detection time are useful operational KPIs, but they measure activity in one area rather than overall risk. Blocked attacks mostly reflect attacker volume and can rise as the program improves."},
{"id":"g36","d":1,"q":"A defense supplier must host a new engineering collaboration system that will store and process ITAR-controlled technical data. Engineers need to view, edit, and run simulations on the data in the cloud. The preferred commercial provider has a FedRAMP High authorization, hosts data in US regions, and uses a follow-the-sun support model with staff in several countries. Which requirement is MOST critical for the hosting design?","o":["The provider must hold a FedRAMP High authorization for the specific services that store and process the technical data","Data must be stored only in US regions, with replication and backups also kept within US data centers throughout","Data must be encrypted end to end with FIPS-validated modules so that its presence in the cloud isn't treated as an export","Only authorized US persons may access or administer the systems, which may require a dedicated government cloud offering"],"a":[3],"e":"Under ITAR, access by foreign persons is an export, so the key requirement is that only authorized US persons can access or administer the data. A follow-the-sun support model breaks that. FedRAMP High doesn't require US-person support. US storage is necessary but not sufficient. The end-to-end encryption carve-out doesn't help when the data has to be decrypted in the cloud to edit and run simulations."},
{"id":"g37","d":1,"q":"A company's new draft 'Encryption Policy,' approved by the CIO, states the board's commitment to protecting data, lists the exact TLS versions and cipher suites allowed, and gives step-by-step instructions for configuring load balancers. Engineering complains that every cipher change needs board-level approval. The document control team uses a three-year review cycle for policies. Which change BEST fixes the structure?","o":["Keep the policy at the level of intent, move the cipher settings into a mandatory standard, and move the steps into procedures","Move the cipher settings and the steps into guidelines so engineering can update them without going through policy approval","Keep everything in the policy but delegate approval of the cipher list to the CISO so changes don't need to go to the board","Split the document into a policy for TLS settings and a separate procedure for the board's commitment and the configuration steps"],"a":[0],"e":"Policies state intent and are approved at a high level. Standards hold specific mandatory rules like allowed ciphers and can change faster. Procedures hold the steps. Guidelines aren't mandatory, so allowed ciphers don't belong there. Delegating approval treats the symptom and keeps detail in the wrong document. Putting the board's commitment in a procedure gets the hierarchy backward."},
{"id":"g38","d":1,"q":"A bank is subject to an operational resilience regulation that requires it to stay within impact tolerances for its important business services during severe but plausible disruptions. The bank has strong DR for individual applications, with recovery time objectives set system by system. Payments depend on a cloud provider, a messaging network, and a mainframe in a second data center. A new CIO wants to modernize the DR tooling. Which activity BEST addresses the regulation?","o":["Tighten the recovery time objective of each system supporting payments so that even the slowest component recovers within the tolerance","Map each important business service end to end, including third parties, and test severe scenarios against its impact tolerance","Increase DR test frequency for the payment applications from yearly to quarterly and report the results to the board","Refresh the business impact analysis for each payment application and update the RTOs to reflect current business volumes"],"a":[1],"e":"Operational resilience is service-centric: you map everything an important business service depends on, including third parties, and test whether it stays within tolerance under severe but plausible scenarios. Tightening system RTOs, more frequent DR tests, and per-application BIAs are all system-centric and can miss third-party and cross-system failures."},
{"id":"g39","d":1,"q":"An organization is choosing a risk assessment approach for its top ten cyber scenarios. The CFO wants results that can be compared directly with capital investments and insurance limits. Internal loss data is thin, but the team can get industry loss data and has staff willing to be trained as calibrated estimators. The current method is a five-by-five heat map. Which approach is MOST suitable?","o":["Keep the five-by-five heat map and attach a dollar band to each impact rating so the CFO can see the rough financial scale","Use NIST SP 800-30 with qualitative likelihood and impact scales, adding written rationale for each rating the team assigns","Use a quantitative model such as FAIR with calibrated ranges and industry data, producing loss distributions in dollar terms","Use OCTAVE Allegro workshops to identify information assets and score each scenario against the organization's defined impact areas"],"a":[2],"e":"The CFO wants numbers comparable with investments and insurance limits, and the team has industry data plus calibrated estimators, which is what FAIR needs to produce dollar loss ranges. A heat map with dollar bands is still ordinal and can't support cost-benefit math. NIST 800-30 used qualitatively and OCTAVE Allegro are both mainly qualitative."},
{"id":"g40","d":1,"q":"An architect is reviewing a contract with a major cloud provider for a workload holding sensitive customer financial data. The standard terms cap the provider's liability at the fees paid in the prior 12 months, about $300K, while the estimated cost of a large breach is over $15M. The provider has said it rarely changes standard terms for customers of this size. Procurement wants to sign this week. What should the architect recommend?","o":["Choose a different provider whose standard terms offer a higher liability cap for customer data breaches","Sign the standard terms as offered, since the provider's security certifications make a breach on its side unlikely","Ask the provider to take unlimited liability for breaches involving the workload, given the sensitivity","Treat most breach cost as retained, so size controls and insurance to it and seek a data breach super-cap"],"a":[3],"e":"With a $300K cap against a $15M exposure, most breach cost stays with the company whatever the contract says. Plan for that: design controls and size insurance to the retained exposure, and still try for a higher cap for data breaches. Switching providers for a slightly higher cap rarely changes the picture. Certifications don't move liability. Unlimited liability isn't a realistic ask of a major provider."},
{"id":"g41","d":1,"q":"A company is setting up a security architecture review board. Some proposals for its charter: having it pen test every release, having it approve project budgets, having it own the enterprise risk register, and having it decide whether designs conform to security principles and standards and rule on deviations. The board will have seven members and meet weekly. Which item should be the board's PRIMARY purpose?","o":["Run or commission security testing on each release so findings are caught before production","Govern whether designs conform to security principles and standards, and decide on deviations","Approve project budgets so that security requirements get funded before projects are approved","Own the enterprise risk register and accept the residual risks that projects bring to the board"],"a":[1],"e":"An architecture review board governs design conformance to principles and standards and decides deviations, escalating risk acceptance where needed. Testing every release is an operational activity. Budget approval belongs to portfolio governance. Risk acceptance belongs to risk owners at the right authority level, not the review board."},
{"id":"g42","d":1,"q":"A company wants to roll out fingerprint time clocks for 2,500 hourly employees in a state whose biometric privacy law requires written notice, written consent before collection, and a published retention and destruction schedule, with statutory damages per violation. The vendor stores templates encrypted and supports on-device matching. HR wants to go live at 12 sites in a month, and unions have asked about alternatives. What should the architect insist on?","o":["Use on-device matching so templates stay on each clock and are not sent to the vendor's central servers","Store templates only as encrypted, salted hashes so the raw fingerprint images can't be reconstructed","Hold go-live until notice, written consent, and a published retention schedule are in place, with an alternative","Complete a data protection impact assessment and have the privacy office sign off on it before the pilot sites begin"],"a":[2],"e":"The law sets preconditions: notice, written consent before collection, and a published retention schedule. Collecting without them creates per-violation damages no matter how well the data is protected. An alternative for people who decline addresses the union concern. On-device matching and template protection are good design but don't meet the legal preconditions. A DPIA is good practice but doesn't satisfy the statute either."},
{"id":"g43","d":1,"q":"A company's privacy office requires a screening review for new projects to decide whether a GDPR Data Protection Impact Assessment is needed. Four proposals arrive the same week from HR, sales, IT, and marketing. All involve EU personal data, two use new vendors, and the privacy team has time for only one full DPIA this month, with the rest getting a lighter review. Which project MOST clearly requires a full DPIA?","o":["Moving payroll to a new SaaS provider with the same data fields and processing as the current system","An AI analytics tool that continuously scores employees' productivity from keystroke and app usage data","A B2B CRM that stores business contact names, job titles, and work email addresses for sales staff","Website analytics on the public site that set cookies only after visitors give consent in the banner"],"a":[1],"e":"Systematic monitoring of employees using new technology is high risk under Article 35 and EDPB criteria: it's systematic, uses new technology, and involves people in a dependent relationship. A like-for-like payroll migration may need a review but not usually a full DPIA. B2B contact data and consented analytics are lower risk."},
{"id":"g44","d":1,"q":"An architect is designing records management for a group with subsidiaries in three countries that require tax records to be kept for 5, 7, and 10 years respectively. Tax records include employee expense claims with personal data, and one subsidiary is in the EU. Finance wants one global rule for simplicity and suggests 10 years everywhere. Storage cost isn't a concern, and the records platform supports per-site policies. What is the BEST approach?","o":["Apply 10 years globally, since it meets each country's minimum and storage cost is low","Apply each jurisdiction's required period to its own records and dispose of them when it ends","Apply a 7-year global period as a compromise and handle the 10-year country's gap with exceptions","Apply 5 years globally and move older records in the other two countries to a separate archive"],"a":[1],"e":"Keeping personal data longer than required raises privacy risk and conflicts with storage limitation, so each jurisdiction's period should apply to its own records with defensible disposal. A 10-year rule over-retains in two countries. A 7-year rule fails the 10-year country without exceptions that recreate complexity. A 5-year rule with a side archive is just jurisdiction-specific retention done badly."},
{"id":"g45","d":1,"q":"An internal audit finds that quarterly access reviews for the claims application are performed but not documented. The application owner insists the reviews happen and offers to sign a statement saying so. The company's IGA tool can run certification campaigns, but it was never set up for this application. The external auditor will rely on internal audit's work at year end. What is the BEST response?","o":["Have the owner sign an attestation covering the past four quarters and file it with the audit workpapers","Contest the finding, since the reviews were done and the issue is only a gap in the paperwork","Redesign the review in the IGA tool so it produces dated evidence of reviewer decisions automatically","Move the reviews from quarterly to monthly to show the auditors a higher level of control rigor"],"a":[2],"e":"An undocumented control can't be shown to operate, so the fix is a process that creates evidence as a byproduct. The IGA tool is already available. A retroactive attestation is weak evidence. Contesting the finding misses the point that evidence is part of the control. More frequent undocumented reviews still leave no evidence."},
{"id":"g46","d":1,"q":"An insurer's enterprise architecture principles now include 'secure by design.' A year later, the CISO wants evidence that the principle is actually built into governance rather than just stated. Candidate evidence includes a SAST tool that blocks high findings in CI, annual secure coding training with 98% completion, a pen test before every major release, and a portfolio process. Which practice MOST directly shows the principle is embedded in governance?","o":["SAST in the CI pipeline that blocks merges with high-severity findings until they're fixed","Security requirements approved at concept stage, with architecture gates projects must pass","A penetration test of each major release, with go-live held until critical findings close","Annual secure coding training for all developers, with completion reported to senior leadership"],"a":[1],"e":"Secure by design means security is set at the start and governance enforces it. Requirements approved at concept with design gates shows exactly that. SAST and pre-release pen tests are valuable but catch problems in code or late in delivery. Training builds skill but doesn't show that governance requires secure design."},
{"id":"g47","d":1,"q":"A risk scenario has an inherent annualized loss expectancy of $800,000. Control A, costing $120,000 a year, cuts the likelihood of the event by 50%. Control B, costing $60,000 a year, reduces the impact of each event that still occurs by 25%. The controls act independently, and the risk owner has asked for the residual figure before deciding whether to fund a third control. Ignoring control costs, what is the residual ALE once both controls are in place?","o":["$200,000","$300,000 a year","$400,000 per year","$420,000 each year"],"a":[1],"e":"Controls applied in sequence multiply: 800,000 x 0.5 = 400,000 after A, then x 0.75 = 300,000 after B. $200,000 adds the percentages (75% off) instead of applying them in turn. $400,000 applies only Control A. $420,000 subtracts the control costs from the original ALE, which mixes cost with risk."},
{"id":"g48","d":1,"q":"A software company tells a federal prime contractor that it 'follows NIST guidance voluntarily.' The prime's subcontract includes a DFARS clause requiring the company to implement NIST SP 800-171 to protect controlled unclassified information. The company also uses NIST CSF 2.0 internally for board reporting. Its counsel asks the architect to explain where the obligations come from. Which statement is MOST accurate?","o":["SP 800-171 is a NIST publication like CSF, so it stays voluntary guidance that the company may tailor as it sees fit","SP 800-171 binds the company through the contract clause, while its use of CSF for board reporting stays voluntary","SP 800-171 has the force of federal regulation for all US companies once NIST publishes it as final","SP 800-171 becomes binding only after the company is certified under CMMC, so until then it's best-effort guidance"],"a":[1],"e":"Frameworks are voluntary on their own but become binding when a law, regulation, or contract adopts them. Here the DFARS clause in the subcontract makes 800-171 a contractual obligation. CSF use for board reporting remains voluntary. NIST publications don't bind companies by being published. The contract obligation exists whether or not a CMMC certification has been issued."},
{"id":"g49","d":1,"q":"A law firm's partners want to let staff use generative AI tools. A quick survey shows about 40% of staff already use free consumer chatbots, some with client documents. The general counsel worries about privilege and client confidentiality. The firm's web proxy can block categories, and its DLP tool can inspect browser uploads. The managing partner wants a decision in two weeks. Which control should the architect recommend FIRST?","o":["Block generative AI categories at the web proxy until a full risk assessment of each tool is complete","Add DLP rules that detect and block client document uploads to known AI services in the browser","An AI acceptable use policy with data rules for each service, backed by approved, contracted tools","Start an ISO/IEC 42001 AI management system project to set up governance across all AI use cases"],"a":[2],"e":"Staff are already using AI, so the first need is clear rules on what data can go where, plus approved tools with contractual protections so people have a safe option. Blocking alone drives use to personal devices. DLP enforces a policy, so it should follow the policy. An ISO 42001 program is a long-term option, far heavier than a two-week decision needs."},
{"id":"g50","d":1,"q":"During a risk assessment at a regional utility, the architect finds that one senior network engineer with 15 years of tenure manages all routers and firewalls, makes changes without peer review, and keeps the only copy of device credentials in a personal password vault. He is highly trusted and has never had a performance issue. Change tickets exist but are approved after the fact. Which risk does this MOST directly represent?","o":["Insider threat, because one person could make malicious changes that no one else is positioned to notice","Key person dependency combined with missing separation of duties and dual control over critical devices","Privilege creep, because one engineer's access has grown over 15 years to cover the entire network estate","Change management failure, because tickets are approved after changes are made instead of before"],"a":[1],"e":"The core issue is concentration: one person holds the only credentials and makes unreviewed changes, so the company depends on him and has no separation of duties or dual control. Insider threat and late change approval are real consequences of that concentration. Privilege creep implies access built up beyond what a role needs, but managing these devices is his job."},
{"id":"g51","d":1,"q":"A company is evaluating a hyperscale cloud provider for a regulated workload. The provider won't allow customer audits of its data centers but offers ISO 27001 and SOC 2 Type II reports and a CSA STAR Level 2 attestation. The company's vendor policy, written ten years ago, requires on-site audits of critical suppliers. The SOC 2 report lists several complementary user entity controls. What is the BEST approach?","o":["Insist on an on-site audit right as the policy requires, and pick another provider if this one won't agree","Rely on the reports after checking their scope, exceptions, and user entity controls, and close gaps in the contract","Send the company's 300-question security questionnaire and require written answers to each item before approving the provider","Accept the certifications as sufficient, since they come from accredited third parties under recognized standards"],"a":[1],"e":"Third-party attestations are the normal assurance model for hyperscale providers, but they need to be read: check that the scope covers the services you use, look at exceptions, and implement the complementary user entity controls. Then close remaining gaps through contract. The old policy should get an exception or update. A long questionnaire adds little over audited reports. Accepting certifications without reading them misses the scope and the user entity controls."},
{"id":"g52","d":1,"q":"A new CISO is asked to judge whether security governance at a retailer is effective. She notes four things: the company has had no audit findings for two years, the security budget grew 30% this year, 99% of staff completed awareness training, and business unit leaders include risk ratings in investment cases and have changed or dropped projects because of them. Which observation is the BEST evidence that governance is effective?","o":["No audit findings for two years across the financial and IT audit programs","The security budget grew 30% this year after the board approved the plan","Business leaders use risk ratings in investment cases and change plans","Awareness training reached 99% completion across the whole workforce"],"a":[2],"e":"Governance works when risk shapes business decisions, and leaders changing projects based on risk shows that. No findings may reflect audit scope rather than good governance. Budget growth shows investment, not results. Training completion is an activity measure."},
{"id":"g53","d":1,"q":"A company's primary data center is in a region exposed to hurricanes. The BIA sets a 24-hour recovery objective for core order processing and five days for everything else. Full active-active across regions would cost $6M up front plus $1.5M a year, which is well above the security budget. The current cyber and property insurance has no business interruption coverage. Which treatment BEST fits?","o":["Build active-active across two regions for all systems, since the event would be catastrophic for the business","Accept the risk formally, since a direct hurricane hit is unlikely in a given year and full mitigation is too costly","Buy business interruption insurance sized to the expected loss and keep the current single-site design","Fund a warm site for order processing to meet the 24-hour target and add insurance for the remaining loss"],"a":[3],"e":"Combining partial mitigation sized to the BIA with insurance for the rest is usually most cost-effective for rare but severe events. A warm site for order processing meets the 24-hour target, and insurance covers the rest. Active-active for everything overshoots the BIA and budget. Acceptance leaves a catastrophic risk above appetite. Insurance alone pays money but doesn't restore order processing within 24 hours."},
{"id":"g54","d":1,"q":"A development team wants a weekly copy of production customer data in the test environment to reproduce hard-to-find defects. Test has weaker access controls, 60 contractors have access, and backups of test aren't encrypted. The team says synthetic data has missed real edge cases twice this year. The DPO has not yet been consulted. What should the architect recommend?","o":["Allow the weekly copy but limit test access to senior employees, removing the 60 contractors while the copy is in place","Encrypt the test database and its backups with managed keys before loading the weekly production copy","Use masked or synthetic data by default, and where real data is needed, apply production-level controls and approval","Get customer consent through a terms update to permit their data to be used in development and testing"],"a":[2],"e":"Default to masked or synthetic data. Where real data truly is needed for a defect, treat that environment like production and get approval, which involves the DPO. Restricting access helps but leaves weak controls in place. Encryption covers storage but not the access problem. Consent through a terms update is unlikely to be freely given or necessary, and it doesn't fix the control gap."},
{"id":"g55","d":1,"q":"A company's main customer database holds names, account numbers, and dates of birth. The breach notification law it falls under exempts data that was encrypted when the key wasn't also compromised. Database servers already use full-disk encryption, and transparent database encryption is available with the key stored on the same server. Most real-world incidents at the company involve stolen admin credentials. Which design MOST directly reduces notification exposure?","o":["Rely on the existing full-disk encryption, since the data is encrypted at rest on each database server","Enable transparent database encryption with the key held locally so the database files and backups are encrypted at rest","Mask account numbers and dates of birth in the application UI so only partial values appear on screen","Encrypt sensitive fields with keys kept in a separate KMS or HSM, under separate admin access and logging"],"a":[3],"e":"The exemption only helps if the key wasn't compromised along with the data. Keeping keys in a separate KMS or HSM with separate admin control makes it more likely that stolen admin credentials or database files don't also expose the keys. Full-disk encryption and locally held TDE keys don't protect against an attacker with admin access to a running system. UI masking doesn't change what's stored or exposed."},
{"id":"g56","d":1,"q":"A 40-person SaaS startup has no formal security program. Two enterprise prospects worth a combined $2M a year are asking for evidence of security, and the CEO wants to 'get compliant.' The budget is about $150K. A compliance automation vendor is offering a discount if the startup signs this month, and a board member suggests ISO 27001 because her last company used it. What should the architect do FIRST?","o":["Sign with the compliance automation vendor this month so that control monitoring and evidence collection can start right away at the discounted price","Find out exactly what assurance the prospects require, such as SOC 2 Type II or ISO 27001, and plan a scoped path to it","Start an ISO 27001 ISMS, since it is internationally recognized and would satisfy most enterprise customers","Adopt NIST SP 800-53 moderate as the control baseline so the program is built on a comprehensive catalog"],"a":[1],"e":"The goal is closing deals, so first find out what assurance these customers want and plan a scoped, affordable route to it. Buying tooling before knowing the target can lock in the wrong path. ISO 27001 may be right, but it isn't automatically what US prospects ask for. 800-53 moderate is far heavier than a 40-person startup needs."},
{"id":"g57","d":1,"q":"Marketing and Sales both claim ownership of a shared customer database. Marketing runs campaigns from it, and Sales enters most of the records. Nobody has approved access requests for four months, so IT has been granting access on the requester's say-so. The security team has offered to own the data to break the deadlock. What is the BEST resolution?","o":["Have senior management assign one accountable owner for classification and access, with others as stakeholders","Make the security team the data owner, since it can apply consistent access decisions and has no business unit bias","Set up joint ownership so both Marketing and Sales must approve each access request before it is granted","Have IT approve access requests by default based on job role until the two business units reach agreement"],"a":[0],"e":"Ownership is a business accountability that senior management assigns to one person, with others consulted. Security is a custodian and advisor, not a business owner. Joint approval spreads accountability and slows access. IT approving by default is what's already happening, and it means no one owns the risk."},
{"id":"g58","d":1,"q":"At a large insurer, the security team keeps its own architecture repository and produces security designs after the enterprise architecture team has finished solution designs. Projects often face late, costly rework, and business sponsors see security as a separate approval step. The EA team uses TOGAF and an ArchiMate repository. Which change BEST reflects how security architecture should relate to enterprise architecture?","o":["Keep the separate security repository but require EA designs to pass a security sign-off before build","Make security a view within the enterprise architecture, aligned to business goals at each phase","Move the security architects into the EA team so all architecture work reports to the chief architect","Have security publish reference designs that the EA team must copy into each solution design"],"a":[1],"e":"Security architecture should be part of the enterprise architecture, aligned to business goals and present from the start of each phase. That's what stops late rework. A sign-off gate keeps security as a separate late step. Changing reporting lines doesn't by itself change when security is considered. Reference designs help but are a tool, not the relationship."},
{"id":"g59","d":1,"q":"A company's internal audit function reports to the CIO, who also owns most of the systems audit reviews. The audit committee has noticed that IT findings tend to be softened before they reach the board, and the CIO controls the audit budget. The head of audit is well regarded, and an external firm co-sources specialist IT audits. What is the BEST recommendation?","o":["Have internal audit report functionally to the audit committee, with administrative reporting to a senior executive","Outsource IT audits fully to the co-source firm while the head of audit continues to report to the CIO for budget and staffing","Move internal audit under the CISO so it reports to someone with security expertise outside IT operations","Rotate audit staff across business areas on a two-year cycle so no auditor gets too close to IT management"],"a":[0],"e":"Independence comes from functional reporting to the audit committee, with a separate administrative line for day-to-day matters. Outsourcing while reporting to the CIO keeps the same conflict. The CISO is also management and owns controls audit reviews. Rotation helps objectivity of individuals but doesn't fix the structural conflict."},
{"id":"g60","d":1,"q":"A security architect is asked to pick one headline metric for the vulnerability management program. Scanning coverage recently rose from 60% to 95% of assets, which roughly doubled the number of findings. The company's highest exposure is a set of internet-facing apps, and the standard sets a 15-day remediation SLA for critical issues on them. Which metric BEST shows how well the company is reducing exposure?","o":["Total number of vulnerabilities found each month across all scanned assets, trended over the last year","Percentage of assets covered by authenticated scanning, trended against the goal of full coverage","Number of open critical vulnerabilities across the estate at month end, compared with last month","Mean time to remediate critical issues on internet-facing assets, measured against the 15-day SLA"],"a":[3],"e":"Remediation speed on the highest-exposure assets against the SLA shows how fast real exposure is closed. Total findings rose because coverage improved, so the count is misleading. Coverage is an important input but not an outcome. Open critical counts across the whole estate mix internal and external exposure and can be skewed by the scanning change."},
{"id":"g61","d":1,"q":"An EU bank's regulator requires threat-led penetration testing of its critical functions every three years, following a TIBER-EU style approach. The bank already runs quarterly vulnerability scans, an annual pen test of its internet apps in a staging environment, and a public bug bounty. Some board members are nervous about any testing that touches production payment systems. Which plan BEST meets the requirement?","o":["Expand the annual staging pen test to cover the critical functions in full and add social engineering to the scope","Run a red team exercise on live production systems for critical functions, driven by sector threat intelligence","Hold a facilitated tabletop based on current threat intelligence, with the board and critical function owners","Extend the bug bounty scope to the payment systems and raise rewards for findings in critical functions"],"a":[1],"e":"Threat-led testing means intelligence-driven red teaming of the live production systems behind critical functions, with a control team to manage the risk. Board concern is handled through that structure rather than by moving off production. A staging pen test doesn't test production. Tabletops test decisions, not controls. A bug bounty isn't intelligence-led and isn't run as a controlled red team exercise."},
{"id":"g62","d":1,"q":"An architect finds that the company's risk register has 400 entries, mostly rated Medium, many with no owner and many describing technical issues like 'TLS 1.0 on server X.' Executives say it's not useful for decisions. The risk team has asked for a GRC tool, and the board's risk appetite statement was approved last year. What is the BEST way to improve the register?","o":["Buy the GRC tool so the 400 risks can be tracked with workflow, reminders, and dashboards for each owner","Re-score all 400 entries on a 1 to 10 scale to separate them better and show the true spread of ratings","Consolidate the entries into business-level risks with named owners and clear criteria tied to appetite","Keep only the top 20 entries in the register and move the rest into the vulnerability management backlog"],"a":[2],"e":"The register fails because it mixes technical findings with risks, lacks owners, and isn't tied to appetite. Consolidating into business-level risks with owners, criteria, and appetite links fixes that. A tool would just automate a poor register. A finer scale doesn't fix the content. Keeping the top 20 throws away the link between technical issues and the business risks they feed."},
{"id":"g63","d":1,"q":"A company has gone cloud-first across AWS, Azure, and Google Cloud, with about 300 accounts and subscriptions owned by different product teams. The board wants assurance that cloud risk is managed consistently. Each provider has its own security center with default benchmarks, and the CIO has floated consolidating onto one provider, which would take four years. Which mechanism is MOST effective?","o":["Rely on each provider's native security center and default benchmark, with monthly summary reports to the board","Consolidate onto one cloud provider so there is a single control set and one team to manage it","Set a cloud policy and standards enforced through policy-as-code guardrails and posture monitoring in all three","Create a separate security team for each provider that writes its own standards and reports to the CISO"],"a":[2],"e":"One set of policy and standards, enforced as code across all three providers with continuous posture monitoring, gives consistent control and evidence for the board. Native centers with default benchmarks differ per provider and aren't tailored to the company's risk. Consolidation may be a valid business decision but takes four years and isn't the architect's call. Separate teams with their own standards produce the inconsistency the board is worried about."},
{"id":"g64","d":1,"q":"An architect must justify a $900K data loss prevention program to the CFO. Last year's proof of concept flagged 4,200 events, most of them low risk. The company had two notable data exposure incidents in three years, and the maximum regulatory fine in its main jurisdiction is 4% of revenue. A competitor's DLP license is cheaper. Which input is MOST relevant to estimating the program's expected benefit?","o":["The number of events the proof of concept flagged last year, showing how much activity the tool can see","The estimated reduction in annualized loss from exfiltration and accidental disclosure, based on the data","The maximum regulatory fine of 4% of revenue, since a single serious incident could trigger that penalty","The license cost difference against the competing product, to show the chosen tool is good value"],"a":[1],"e":"Expected benefit is the reduction in annualized loss the program delivers, built from incident history, industry data, and the program's expected effect. Flagged events are activity, and most were low risk. The maximum fine is a worst case, not an expected value. License comparison is about cost, not benefit."},
{"id":"g65","d":1,"q":"A company in a regulated sector wants to adopt a SaaS analytics tool. The vendor's DPA allows it to use subprocessors with 30 days' notice, and its current list includes a support subprocessor in a country with weak privacy protections and broad government access powers. The business sponsor says the vendor is the only one with the needed features, and the vendor holds ISO 27701. Which action is MOST important?","o":["Reject the vendor, since data reaching a weak-privacy country creates exposure the company can't accept under its policy","Accept the vendor's DPA, since ISO 27701 certification shows that privacy controls are already in place","Encrypt the data client-side before it's uploaded so no subprocessor can read it wherever it sits","Review the subprocessor's data access and risk, then require safeguards, transfer terms, and objection rights"],"a":[3],"e":"The right step is to understand what the support subprocessor can actually access, assess that risk, and require safeguards such as transfer terms, limits on access, and a right to object to new subprocessors. Rejecting the only suitable vendor is premature before the risk is understood. ISO 27701 doesn't address this specific transfer. Client-side encryption would break the analytics the tool exists to provide."},
{"id":"g66","d":1,"q":"A company has adopted the IIA's Three Lines Model. Its security architecture team designs and builds controls for project teams and also writes the enterprise security standards that other teams must follow. Internal audit wants to rely on the architecture team's design reviews as independent assurance. The CRO asks where the architecture team fits. What is the BEST answer?","o":["Third line, since its design reviews are independent of project teams and can serve as assurance","Second line only, since writing enterprise standards is a risk oversight function for the company","First or second line depending on the activity, and not a source of independent assurance","Outside the model, since architecture is an advisory function and doesn't own or oversee risks"],"a":[2],"e":"Building controls is first-line work and setting standards is second-line work, so the team sits in one or both depending on the activity. Either way it's part of management, so internal audit, the third line, shouldn't treat its reviews as independent assurance. It isn't outside the model either."},
{"id":"g67","d":1,"q":"A health insurer is building a new claims system. The privacy officer insists on privacy by design. The project team proposes four measures: encrypting all fields at rest, updating the privacy notice to describe the new system, adding annual privacy training for claims staff, and limiting collected fields to those needed to decide claims, with defaults that restrict secondary use. The budget allows only one of these this release. Which measure MOST directly demonstrates privacy by design?","o":["Encrypt each field at rest in the claims database using keys from the central key service","Update the privacy notice to explain how the new claims system collects and uses member data","Collect only the fields needed to decide claims, with defaults that restrict secondary uses","Add annual privacy training for claims staff with a module on the new system's data handling"],"a":[2],"e":"Privacy by design is about building privacy into the system itself, especially data minimization and privacy-protective defaults. Encryption is a security control that protects data you may not need to hold. A notice and training are important but sit outside the design."},
{"id":"g68","d":1,"q":"A pharmaceutical company keeps its crown-jewel formulation data in a document management system. An assessment finds 3,000 employees have read access, but only about 200 need it. The company recently bought a DLP tool and a UEBA platform, and the DMS supports group-based permissions. The research VP worries that cutting access will slow collaboration. What is the BEST risk treatment from an architecture view?","o":["Turn on DLP policies for the formulation documents so they can't be emailed or uploaded outside the company","Feed DMS access logs into UEBA to detect unusual reading patterns across the 3,000 current users","Restrict access to the roughly 200 who need it, monitor that access, and have data owners review it","Classify and label the formulation documents so users understand their sensitivity before they share"],"a":[2],"e":"Cutting access to those who need it shrinks exposure at the source, which matters most for crown-jewel data. Monitoring and owner review then keep it right, and a request path addresses the collaboration concern. DLP, UEBA, and labeling are useful layers but leave 2,800 people with access they don't need."},
{"id":"g69","d":1,"q":"An architect is building a three-year security roadmap. Inputs available include a maturity assessment showing gaps across all CSF functions, a vendor threat report, last year's budget, and the business strategy, which includes launching a payments business and moving 70% of workloads to cloud. The CFO has signaled a flat budget. Which input should drive prioritization MOST?","o":["The maturity assessment, prioritizing the CSF functions with the largest gaps between current and target","The vendor threat report, prioritizing defenses against the attack techniques trending in the sector","The flat budget, prioritizing low-cost improvements that fit within last year's spending envelope","The business strategy and risk profile, including the payments launch and the cloud migration"],"a":[3],"e":"The roadmap should enable and protect what the business plans to do, so payments and cloud should shape priority. Maturity gaps, threat trends, and budget all inform the plan, but on their own they can push effort toward areas the business is moving away from."},
{"id":"g70","d":1,"q":"A private equity-backed services company plans to buy six firms over three years, ranging from a 20-person consultancy to a 900-person managed services provider with its own SOC. Their technologies and maturity differ widely. The board wants consistent security outcomes without stalling deals or integration. The first deal, for the managed services provider, closes in 60 days, and the parent's own program is rated mature. Which governance mechanism is BEST?","o":["Require each target to meet all of the parent's security standards before the deal closes","Let each acquired firm keep running its own program for two years before integration starts","Migrate each firm onto the parent's technology stack in its first 90 days after the deal closes","An integration playbook with minimum baselines, risk-based timelines, and checks before connectivity"],"a":[3],"e":"A playbook gives a repeatable approach that scales across very different firms: minimum baselines, timelines set by risk, and checkpoints before deeper connection. Full compliance before close would stall deals and isn't in the parent's control. Two years of independence leaves risk unmanaged. Forcing a 90-day migration ignores size and maturity differences, such as the target that already runs a SOC."},
{"id":"g71","d":1,"q":"A bank is outsourcing development of a customer mobile app to an offshore firm. The bank's internal secure SDLC policy runs to 60 pages and is published on its intranet. The statement of work lists features and delivery dates, and the firm has signed the bank's standard NDA. Which document BEST communicates the security requirements the firm must follow?","o":["The bank's secure SDLC policy, shared through the intranet portal with an acknowledgment by the firm","The statement of work, extended so each feature lists its security acceptance criteria and test cases","A schedule referenced in the contract setting out secure development, testing, and remediation duties","The NDA, amended to add clauses covering handling of source code and the bank's customer test data"],"a":[2],"e":"Requirements bind a supplier when they are in the contract, and a contract schedule can cover secure development, testing, remediation, and acceptance in one place. An internal policy isn't binding on a third party. Feature-level criteria in the SOW help but don't cover the development process. An NDA covers confidentiality, not secure development."},
{"id":"g72","d":1,"q":"A multinational SaaS provider gets several government requests a month for customer data, including requests under the US CLOUD Act and from EU and Asian authorities. Some requests conflict with customers' local privacy laws. Engineering has proposed customer-held keys for some tiers, and sales says customers ask about this in every deal. Which design element is MOST important?","o":["Customer-held keys across the service tiers so the provider can't produce readable data when a government asks","Prompt fulfillment of requests from recognized authorities to keep the provider in good legal standing","A documented process with legal review, minimal disclosure, customer notice where lawful, and reporting","Routing each request to the country manager where the data sits so local staff can decide how to respond"],"a":[2],"e":"A documented process with legal review checks validity and conflicts, limits disclosure to what's required, notifies customers where allowed, and supports transparency reporting. Customer-held keys are a useful option for some tiers but don't replace a process and don't fit every service. Prompt compliance without review can breach other laws. Country managers lack the legal expertise and consistency needed."},
{"id":"g73","d":1,"q":"Two years ago a business owner formally accepted the risk of running an internet-facing file transfer server with a known medium-severity flaw, with a review set for every two years. Last week, exploit code for the flaw was published and a ransomware group began using it. The original acceptance memo is signed and on file. Which statement about the acceptance is MOST accurate?","o":["It stays valid until its scheduled review, since it was signed by an owner with the right authority","It should be re-evaluated now, because the conditions it was based on have changed materially","It transfers to the security team once exploitation begins, since active threats are a security matter","It can be renewed by the security team without the owner, since only the likelihood rating changed"],"a":[1],"e":"Acceptance is tied to the conditions at the time, and active exploitation materially changes the likelihood, so the acceptance must be re-evaluated now rather than at the scheduled review. Ownership of the risk stays with the business owner. Security can't renew it on the owner's behalf."},
{"id":"g74","d":1,"q":"A payment service provider is preparing for PCI DSS 4.0. For one requirement, its architecture uses a different control design than the defined approach describes, and the design isn't driven by a technical or business constraint. The QSA has said the customized approach may be used for this requirement. The assessment is in five months, and the company hasn't used the customized approach before. What does that mean for the architect?","o":["Document the design on a compensating control worksheet showing how it meets the intent and rigor of the requirement","Meet the requirement's stated objective with its own design, backed by a targeted risk analysis and a controls matrix","Ask the QSA to design and approve a control that meets the requirement, since the customized approach is assessor-led","Treat the requirement as not applicable, since the customized approach replaces the requirement with the entity's own control"],"a":[1],"e":"The customized approach lets an entity meet a requirement's stated objective with its own control design, documented with a targeted risk analysis and controls matrix, which the assessor then tests. Compensating controls are for legitimate constraints, which don't exist here. The entity designs the control, not the QSA. The requirement still applies; only the way of meeting it changes."},
{"id":"g75","d":1,"q":"An architect is assessing a cloud HR system against the requirement that 'personnel data must not be accessible to the provider's support staff without customer approval.' The provider has a SOC 2 Type II report, offers optional customer-managed keys, and has a support model where engineers can access tenants to fix issues. The system has to stay fully functional as SaaS. Which provider capability BEST meets the requirement?","o":["A SOC 2 Type II report showing that the provider restricts and reviews support staff access to tenant data","A contract clause stating that support staff may access personnel data only with the customer's permission","An approval workflow for support access, such as customer lockbox, with customer-managed keys and access logging","Client-side encryption of all personnel data before upload so support staff only ever see encrypted values"],"a":[2],"e":"A lockbox-style approval workflow technically enforces customer approval, customer-managed keys add a way to revoke, and logging gives evidence. A SOC 2 report shows the provider's own restrictions, not customer approval. A contract clause states the rule without enforcing it. Client-side encryption of all data would break the SaaS application's functions."},
{"id":"g76","d":1,"q":"A CISO wants to know how mature the company's security processes are so she can set a realistic two-year improvement target that the board will accept. The company passed an ISO 27001 surveillance audit last month, ran an external pen test in the spring, and has peer benchmark data on security spending. Several processes are run informally by one or two experienced people. Which approach is MOST appropriate for this purpose?","o":["Use a capability maturity model, such as CMMI levels or NIST CSF tiers, to rate processes and set targets","Run a gap assessment against ISO 27001 Annex A to find controls that are missing or only partly in place","Use the external pen test findings to rate how well each control area is working against real attacks","Compare security spending to peer benchmarks to estimate where the company sits relative to similar firms"],"a":[0],"e":"Maturity models rate how well processes are defined, repeatable, and managed, which is what's needed to set a realistic target. They also expose processes that depend on one or two people. An Annex A gap check is about whether controls exist, and the company just passed its audit. Pen tests measure technical exposure. Spending benchmarks measure investment, not maturity."},
{"id":"g77","d":1,"q":"A company's risk policy says business unit heads may accept risks rated up to Medium, the executive risk committee may accept High, and only the board risk committee may accept Critical. A business owner wants to deploy a new platform that the security team rated High, above the business unit's appetite, arguing that $5M in new revenue justifies it. The CISO disagrees with the deployment. Who should make the final decision?","o":["The CISO, since security assessed the risk and is accountable for the security program","The business owner, since they own the platform and are accountable for its revenue","The executive risk committee, since the policy gives it authority for High risks","The board risk committee, since the risk is above the business unit's appetite"],"a":[2],"e":"Authority follows the policy: High risks go to the executive risk committee. The CISO advises but doesn't own business risk. The business owner's authority stops at Medium. The board risk committee is reserved for Critical risks under this policy, so sending a High risk there skips the delegated authority."},
{"id":"g78","d":1,"q":"A financial firm falls under a cybersecurity regulation requiring an annual compliance certification signed by the CEO and the CISO. The CEO has asked how she can sign in good faith. The firm has an ISO 27001 certificate, an annual external pen test, and controls spread across three business units that track evidence in spreadsheets. Which capability BEST supports the certification?","o":["An annual external penetration test across the business units, with findings reported to the CEO","A control assurance program that collects evidence of control operation and reports exceptions upward","Renewal of the ISO 27001 certificate each cycle, with the certificate attached to the attestation","A delegation letter allowing the CISO to sign on the CEO's behalf because of their technical expertise"],"a":[1],"e":"A good-faith certification needs evidence that controls operate across the firm, with exceptions surfaced to leadership. A control assurance program provides that. A pen test covers only technical exposure at a point in time. An ISO certificate may not cover all the regulation's requirements or scope. The rule requires the CEO's own signature, so delegation doesn't work."},
{"id":"g79","d":1,"q":"An architect is applying SABSA to a new digital banking platform. Stakeholders have shared business goals, regulatory obligations, a list of required security attributes such as 'available' and 'auditable,' a set of chosen logical services, and preferred products. The architect wants to record why the business needs the platform and the risks it faces before anything else. Which SABSA layer is this?","o":["The contextual layer, which captures the business view, its drivers, and the risks it faces","The conceptual layer, which captures the attributes profile and the security objectives","The logical layer, which defines the security services and policies the design will use","The component layer, which identifies the specific products and tools to be deployed"],"a":[0],"e":"The contextual layer is the business view: what the business does, its drivers, and its risks. The conceptual layer turns those into attributes and objectives. The logical layer defines services and policies. The component layer covers products. The scenario mentions all of these, but the architect wants the starting business context."},
{"id":"g80","d":1,"q":"A logistics company's risk committee is reviewing treatments proposed for its customer portal. The four proposals: deploy a WAF in front of the portal, drop plans to launch the portal in one country with strict data laws, buy cyber insurance and sign a hosting contract with breach indemnity, and set aside a cash reserve to pay for incidents itself. Which proposal BEST illustrates risk transference?","o":["Deploying a web application firewall to block common attacks against the portal","Dropping the launch in the country with strict data laws to avoid that exposure","Buying cyber insurance and a hosting contract with indemnity for provider breaches","Setting aside a cash reserve so the company can pay for incident costs on its own"],"a":[2],"e":"Transference shifts the financial impact to another party, which insurance and contractual indemnity both do. Accountability and reputational harm stay with the company. A WAF is mitigation, dropping the launch is avoidance, and a self-funded reserve is retention, a form of acceptance."},
{"id":"g81","d":1,"q":"A regulator asks an insurer to show that its access controls are effective, not just present. The insurer has an approved access control policy, screenshots of current IAM configuration, a management self-assessment rated 'effective,' and an internal audit test that sampled 60 joiner, mover, and leaver events over nine months and checked each against the standard. Which evidence is STRONGEST?","o":["The approved access control policy, showing that the board has set requirements for access","Current IAM configuration screenshots, showing the controls are set up as the standard requires","The management self-assessment rating the controls effective, signed by each control owner","Internal audit's sample test of joiner, mover, and leaver events across nine months"],"a":[3],"e":"Independent testing of a sample over a period shows the control actually operated as intended, which is effectiveness. A policy shows intent. Screenshots show configuration at one moment. A self-assessment is management grading itself."},
{"id":"g82","d":1,"q":"Product teams complain that the cloud security review takes eight weeks and blocks delivery. Most requests are for common patterns such as serverless APIs, managed databases, and object storage, and a few involve novel services. The security team has two reviewers and no headcount budget, and the platform team already maintains landing zone guardrails. Which change BEST balances speed and risk?","o":["Pre-approve standard patterns built on the guardrails for a fast track, and keep full reviews for novel services","Drop reviews for services that cost less than a set monthly amount, since low monthly spend usually means low data and business risk","Replace reviews with a self-attestation questionnaire that teams complete and security checks at random","Ask for two more reviewers in next year's budget and add a service level target for review turnaround"],"a":[0],"e":"Pre-approved patterns with guardrails let common requests move quickly while novel services still get full review. Cost doesn't track risk. Self-attestation without guardrails weakens control. More reviewers isn't available this year and still treats every request the same way."},
{"id":"g83","d":1,"q":"An education technology company runs a learning app used by children aged 8 to 15 in the US and EU. It already encrypts data in transit and at rest and hosts EU data in an EU region. Several jurisdictions where it operates have specific rules on children's data. The product team wants to add a social feed and personalized recommendations. Which requirement is MOST likely to drive architectural change?","o":["Encrypting children's data at rest with separate keys from the adult teacher accounts on the platform","Verifiable parental consent before collection, high-privacy defaults, and limits on profiling and sharing","An age gate asking users to enter a birth date so younger users can be routed to a simpler experience","Hosting US children's data in a US region separate from the EU region used for European users"],"a":[1],"e":"COPPA, GDPR Article 8, and age-appropriate design codes drive verifiable parental consent, high-privacy defaults, and limits on profiling, which shape how the social feed and recommendations are built. Encryption and EU hosting are already done. A self-declared birth date is a weak age check that doesn't satisfy verifiable consent. There's no general rule requiring separate US hosting."},
{"id":"g84","d":1,"q":"An architecture team has seen two key designs reversed by new staff who didn't know why they were chosen, causing outages and a repeat audit finding. The team keeps current diagrams on a wiki, ARB meeting minutes in a shared drive, and design discussions in ticket comments. The CISO wants decisions and their risk rationale preserved for future teams and auditors. Which practice is MOST effective?","o":["Keep the wiki diagrams up to date with a change log showing who edited each one and when","Store ARB meeting minutes in a searchable records system with a defined retention period","Require architecture decision records with context, options, the decision, and its risk rationale","Tag ticket comments that contain design discussion so they can be found later through search"],"a":[2],"e":"Architecture decision records capture the why: context, options considered, the decision, its consequences, and risk rationale, in a form that lasts. Diagrams show the result but not the reasoning. Minutes and ticket comments are scattered and rarely complete enough to rely on."},
{"id":"g85","d":1,"q":"A financial regulator finds that a broker-dealer onboarded a critical market data supplier without reviewing its security, and never checked the supplier afterward. The supplier was later breached, exposing the broker-dealer's customer data. On its own side, the broker-dealer ran strong controls: encryption, MFA, and monitoring. Which principle has the broker-dealer MOST likely violated?","o":["Due care, since it failed to protect customer data that ended up exposed through its supplier","Defense in depth, since a single supplier failure was enough to expose its customer data","Due diligence, since it never investigated or monitored the supplier's security posture","Least privilege, since the supplier seems to have held more customer data than it needed"],"a":[2],"e":"Due diligence is the investigation and ongoing checking that informs decisions, such as assessing a supplier before onboarding and monitoring it after. The firm skipped that. Its own controls show due care on its side. Defense in depth and least privilege might be relevant to the design, but the regulator's finding is about failing to assess the supplier."}
);
